welcome to the Macmillan report I'm  Marilyn Wilkes your host and our guest  Islam and sauna the D Willis James  professor of missions and world  Christianity at the Yale Divinity School  a professor of history and the director  of the project on religious freedom and  society in Africa at the Macmillan  Center he is the author of more than 200  articles on religious and historical  subjects and of several books including  disciples of nations pillars of world  Christianity today we'll talk with  Professor Sanne about his new book  Beyond jihad  welcome professor sana thank you I'm  delighted to be here let's begin with an  overview of your book tell us about it  well the book beyond jihad is about the  story of a pacifist movement in West  African Islam that started eight nine  hundred years ago and my interest in the  subject is to try and discover what held  the group together over such a long  period of time they rejected jihad and  how did they survive the threat of jihad  when they came upon it and in the  process I was also interested in  accounting for the history of the Mali  Empire which is one of the most  spectacular kingdoms in West Africa  founded about 1200 AD and survived until  the end of the sixteenth century and  there all of these religious scholars  who were pacifists within the Empire was  also something I was very very  interested in okay and let's define the  term jihad what do you mean by that  of course jihad is used in two special  ways in it  one way of defining jihad jihad and in  Arabic means struggle or effort the way  it's used in the Quran in chapter 9 of  the Quran it has a military militant  connotation religious scholars have  defined jihad as an internal struggle  kind of spiritual discipline an effort  to Center one's will in God and to be in  a very sort of strong moral position  visibly the attractions of the world and  spiritual jihad is actually called by  the scholars the greater jihad and the  military or militant jihad is called the  lesser jihad but I use it historically  I'm not really involved in a kind of  theological examination of the update of  the idea I'm entering the historical  uses of jihad in West African Islam ok  let's talk about the methodology how did  you do the research for the book well I  have to say that no one had written a  book on the subject before and when I  was in graduate school my thesis  director dissertation supervisor in  American language asked me if I knew  anything about this pacifist group in  African Islam I said no but I said no I  meant two things first I don't know them  and I'm not really sure I want to know  them because such a group sounds to me  incredulous I couldn't believe that  there was a pacifist movement anywhere  in Islam let alone in Africanist why is  that well because I was familiar with  the role of war and warfare in the  origins of Islam the famous book  Montgomery what road is called Mohammed  prophet and statesman and so my idea for  me growing up and learning about Islam  without Islam was both religion and  state and a political system and the  idea of pacifism  sort of begins to suggest that there was  some sort of separation between religion  and politics between church and state  and really I had no intellectual  foundation to grasp that idea at all  so that's why I was reluctant and also  incredulous at the idea so how did you  go about doing the research for the book  I discovered to my embarrassment that  actually I did know something about this  tradition I only denied it and  suppressed it so in the first place I  looked at what scholars may have written  about the subject mostly French and I  found a few articles scattered here and  there and some Western scholars who've  written about this particular group  called the Jahangir clerics as a kind of  commercial end clave and that again  struck me as a little bit odd because  they were really specialists in religion  and commerce was not their specialty  anyway I read up on that and then I went  to the archives went to Paris and looked  at the colonial archives and after all  of that I came back organized my notes  and then went into the field to do  research among the people themselves and  really there is no shortcut there is no  alternative to doing fieldwork to get to  know these people in this community  because that was the only way I really  could overcome my sort of reservations  about the subject where were you I'm  doing the fieldwork well I started in  Senegal and because the groups are based  really in Senegal and then I'm going to  Guinea but the main branch of the  community had moved from Guinea to  Senegal so I interviewed them in Senegal  and then when I was in Ghana I looked at  some of the archives Arabic manuscripts  and then I discovered an Arabic - script  that had been actually translated and  published in a journal in cano in  Nigeria so that was extremely helpful to  me  and so on in what way was it helpful  well because it showed it's a kind of  Arabic Chronicle that describes a branch  of these same people that I was studying  who had migrated from what today would  be money across tucano in Nigeria in the  15th century and that gave me the hint  that actually this is a very old ancient  community and I look at the two famous  chronicles that most historians know  about history of song-i two famous  chronicles and they also describe these  communities going back to the 15th  century and then I found a Portuguese  source  Tiago Gomez who wrote about this  community in the 1450s long before  Columbus Wow so I was really I felt okay  there's solid historical support for  really describing this community as a  clerical community dedicated to religion  opposed to war and warfare and unwilling  to be political rulers themselves  because you know today the words jihad  and Islam carries such a connotation of  violence and historically as you're  saying that's not the case how do you  think it evolved to get to that point  well I don't know I'm only speculating  here because I'm really pedestrian  historian political scientists would  have an angle on this which will be very  illuminating but I think in the 19th  century when the Muslim world after the  caliphate 1928 really the end of the  caliphate by which time the muslim world  had encountered colonial rule and  colonial rule organised the Muslim world  intonation States and at the end of the  Ottoman Caliphate in the 1920s  nation-states were created in the Arab  world Saudi Arabia for example  came into being and various Syria Iraq  and so these national state  jurisdictions elevated whole idea of  Islam to a political level and so it's  very easy I think in the 20th century  and 21st century for people to think Oh  Islam is politics and politics Islam and  I don't think that's really quite  accurate if you look at Sharia for  example which has a very bad name in the  West Sharia rule is intolerant and  bigoted and violent and cutting of limbs  and suppressing women Sharia  paradoxically was created founded by  scholars all of whom were persecuted by  the political rulers all of them and  they were all independent of politics  and the great law schools in Islam were  all independent of political dynasties  so this little group in West Africa  opened my eyes to the wider picture of  how Islam conceives of itself as a  religious mandate rather than as a  political construct so basically what  you conclude in your book what I  conclude in my book is that there is not  exactly a tradition of separation of  church and state in Islam because that's  going too far  there are no organized institutions  where religion belongs under the  leadership of religious masters and  politics belongs to another realm under  the leadership of political masters but  that there is a distinction in Islamic  thought between politics and religion  that political power is separated in  Islamic thought from religious authority  I mean religious authority really  depends on moral authority and moral  influence there is no Pope no Vatican in  Islam Islam is the least bureaucratic of  religions mosque don't have membership  lists until maybe recently and there is  no ordained clergy as such at least in  Sunni Islam  and so Islam strikes me as very  democratic in his view of the membership  of the community everyone is equal in  the eyes of God the law of God is the  same for everyone whether you're a king  or commoner ruler poor Rachel strong or  weak so it seems to me that this is very  compatible with the American idea of  religion as an institution of civil  society so that Islam belongs in civil  society more than it does in the state  and I think in conversations in America  we can appropriate some of these ideas  after all they created the founders of  the American Republic Jefferson John  Adams James Madison Benjamin Franklin  Thomas Jefferson they're all actually  we're aware of Islam and given America's  constitution of no prescription for  religion they said we would welcome a  Mufti from Constantinople really  Istanbul in our pulpit Benjamin Franklin  said they will be welcome in our meeting  house in Philadelphia Jefferson himself  welcomed Muslims in the White House so  it seems to me that if we get over the  present political hub and all this sort  of extreme rhetoric I think we can  appreciate how really this period of  Islam as a civil institution is not  incompatible with American ideas of  freedom of religion yes that would be  nice if that could happen if there was  one thing that you'd like people to take  away from reading your book what would  it be well the first thing I think I  would like people to take away  we are very political in America and  almost every idea we have we want to  sort of express it in political terms  but I think there is a case to be made  for understanding religion as a matter  of flag individual rights but really a  matter of the conscience  the great hairy gentleman in the Puritan  heritage really developed very strong  social institutions which describe  religion as a matter of individual  freedom of conscience in Islam I think  this idea is not so alien  you cannot worship God if you are  compelled to worship God because that's  really not worship anymore there's  something else and you can't love your  neighbour if you are on the dress now it  seems to me that civil society needs  values that the state cannot produce the  state can use those values but it cannot  produce them and so we have a  responsibility in religious communities  to train children in the ethics and the  virtues of truthfulness honesty hard  work concern and sensitivity to the  neighbour and so for and so on and those  are really civil values are not  necessarily state values this has been  very interesting thank you so much for  being here with us today and sharing  some of your work thank you very much  for having me I enjoy being here for  more information about professor sana  and his research please visit our  website at macmillan report yale.edu be  sure to join us again for another  episode of the Macmillan report made  possible through funding from the  Whitney and Betty Macmillan Centre for  International and area studies at Yale
 

